'''Prehistoric Yanbaru''' refers to the period from the first human settlement of the [[Yanbaru]] archipelago approximately 50,000 years ago to the earliest written records in 150 BCE, when the [[Kingdom of Donghai]] first documented the northern island tribes as tribute-paying polities. The prehistoric period encompasses the initial Paleolithic settlement by maritime voyagers from the [[Fosia|Fosian mainland]], the subsequent Neolithic expansion from [[Okaiken]], and the development of the distinct '''Nakashima Culture''' characterized by maritime adaptation, coral reef exploitation, and the foundations of the region's unique cultural identity.

== Settlement ==

=== First settlement (~50,000–15,000 BP) ===
The earliest human presence in Yanbaru dates to approximately 50,000 BP, coinciding with the broader settlement of the [[Sanese Archipelago]] and the [[Jade Peninsula]] region on the Fosian mainland. Unlike the southern regions of Fosia, which were connected to the mainland during periods of lower sea levels, the Yanbaru archipelago was never connected by land bridges. The first settlers arrived by sea, navigating across the straits from the [[Jade Peninsula]] in small watercraft.

These Paleolithic maritime voyagers were hunter-gatherers who exploited the archipelago's rich coastal resources. Archaeological evidence from early sites on [[Nakashima Island]] indicates a diet heavily dependent on marine resources, including shellfish, fish, and sea turtles, supplemented by tropical forest foraging. The warm, equatorial climate of the northern archipelago provided a stable resource base that supported continuous occupation throughout the Paleolithic period.

=== Second wave (~15,000–3,000 BCE) ===
During the Neolithic period, a second wave of migrants arrived from the central Sanese islands to the south, particularly from the region that would later become [[Okaiken]]. This migration was driven by population expansion and maritime exploration from the temperate central islands into the warmer northern latitudes.

The second wave did not displace the existing Paleolithic population. Instead, the two groups intermixed, creating a blended cultural and genetic foundation for the indigenous Yanbaru people. This admixture is evident in the archaeological record, which shows continuity in settlement patterns alongside the introduction of new technologies and cultural practices from the central islands.

== Nakashima Culture ==

The '''Nakashima Culture''' ({{circa|15,000|300|BCE}}) is the archaeological tradition associated with prehistoric Yanbaru. Named after the largest island in the archipelago, the culture is distinguished by its unique maritime adaptations and exploitation of coral reef ecosystems.

=== Subsistence ===
The Nakashima Culture developed specialized techniques for harvesting the resources of the warm, shallow coral reefs surrounding the islands. Unlike the temperate-zone maritime cultures of the central Sanese islands, which focused on deep-water fishing and cold-current species, the Nakashima Culture centered its subsistence strategy on coral reef fisheries, employing complex techniques for harvesting reef fish, crustaceans, and mollusks. The culture also collected tortoiseshell and red coral for tool-making and ornamentation, practiced pearl harvesting through diving in warm lagoons, and exploited dense tropical hardwood forests for construction and woodworking. The culture developed early forms of tropical horticulture as well, cultivating root vegetables and fruit trees suited to the equatorial climate.

=== Material culture ===
Archaeological sites from the Nakashima Culture period reveal distinctive material traditions. The culture produced shell tools including adzes, scrapers, and fishhooks crafted from giant clam and conch shells, as well as coral artifacts utilizing porous coral for abrading tools and ceremonial objects. Early evidence of outrigger canoe technology, adapted for navigation through coral reefs and mangrove channels, has been recovered from coastal sites. The Nakashima Culture also produced distinctive red-slipped ceramics with impressed cord markings, differing significantly from the pottery styles of central Sanese Neolithic cultures.

=== Settlement patterns ===
Nakashima Culture settlements were concentrated along coastal margins, with the densest occupation occurring on coral lagoon shores offering protected waters suitable for canoe storage and reef access, mangrove estuaries providing rich harvesting grounds for shellfish and crustaceans, and river mouths allowing access to both marine and forest resources. Archaeological evidence suggests a pattern of seasonal movement between coastal harvesting sites and inland forest zones, though permanent settlements became more common in the later Neolithic period.

=== Social organization ===
The Nakashima Culture appears to have been organized into small, coastal-dwelling groups likely organized along kinship lines. The absence of large defensive structures or weapons of war in early sites suggests relatively peaceful relations between groups, possibly facilitated by the abundance of marine resources and the development of trade networks.

By the late Neolithic period, evidence of social differentiation appears in the archaeological record. Differential burial practices indicate that some individuals were interred with elaborate grave goods, including shell ornaments, coral artifacts, and exotic materials, while specialized production areas dedicated to shell-tool production and canoe building suggest the emergence of craft specialization. The presence of materials from distant sources, including obsidian from the central islands and exotic shells from deeper waters, indicates the development of trade networks connecting the Yanbaru archipelago to broader Fosian exchange systems.

== End of prehistory ==

=== Contact with eastern coastal polities ===
By the late first millennium BCE, the Nakashima Culture came into increasing contact with polities on the eastern coast of Fosia. The [[Kingdom of Donghai]] had begun expanding its maritime reach eastward.

The earliest written records mentioning the Yanbaru islands date to approximately '''150 BCE''', when Donghai scribes documented the northern island tribes as tribute-paying polities. These records describe a fragmented political landscape of coastal chiefdoms engaged in the harvest and trade of tropical luxury goods—tortoiseshell, red coral, pearls, and tropical hardwoods—in exchange for manufactured goods from the mainland.

=== Transition to ancient period ===
The period following 150 BCE is considered the beginning of the '''Ancient Yanbaru''' period, characterized by the formalization of trade relations with eastern Fosian polities, the political consolidation of coastal chiefdoms into larger entities, the introduction of metalworking from mainland sources, and the development of social stratification and early state formation. The coral chiefdoms described in later historical sources trace their origins to this transitional period, when the Nakashima Culture's maritime traditions combined with external trade influences to create the foundations of Yanbaru's distinctive civilization.

== Archaeological sites ==

=== Early Paleolithic sites ===
The oldest dated site in the archipelago is '''Hedo Point''' on northern Nakashima, with evidence of early coastal foraging dating to approximately 48,000 BP. The nearby '''Cape Hedo Shell Midden''' represents a large midden deposit spanning 40,000–15,000 BP, providing a continuous record of Paleolithic coastal exploitation.

=== Nakashima Culture sites ===
The '''Yomitan Bay Complex''' is an extensive Neolithic settlement containing canoe sheds, shell-tool workshops, and burial areas. The '''Agarihama Lagoon Sites''' comprise a concentration of coral-reef fishing stations and pearl-harvesting camps along the eastern coast of Nakashima. On the island of Irikashima, the '''Irikashima Cliff Shelters''' have preserved organic materials including woven mats and wooden implements. The '''Nakabaru Middens''' represent a massive shell midden complex spanning the entire Nakashima Culture period, providing evidence of continuous occupation and resource exploitation.

